2025-05-01 01:47:56 - Thread-447 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their possible use  

ORDERS  
• "user_id": identifies each customer; primary key for user‑level grouping.  
• "order_id": identifies a unique purchase event; used for de‑duplication.  
• "created_at": micro‑second epoch time of the order; used to define (1) the customer’s first purchase month and (2) the month offsets (month 1, month 2 …).  
• "status": lets us keep only real purchases (usually ‘Complete’, ‘Shipped’, ‘Processing’).  
• "delivered_at", "returned_at": optional filters; may be checked to be sure an order was not returned.  

ORDER_ITEMS  
• "user_id": second route to the customer id (helpful when an order lacks the id).  
• "created_at": time stamp on the line‑item; sometimes safer than the order header.  
• "status": same idea as in ORDERS.  

USERS  
• "id": joins to ORDERS / ORDER_ITEMS when we want demographics or to ensure the user exists.  

Typical conditions you will use  
• "created_at" < 1672531200000000   -- everything up to 31‑Dec‑2022 23:59:59 UTC  
• "status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')   -- keep only real purchases  
• Use DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ("created_at"/1e6)) to isolate a purchase month.  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL statements (progressing from simple to complex) that help you look at the data you will need for the requested retention calculation.  
All column names are wrapped in double quotes, each query is annotated, and every query is capped with LIMIT 20.

```sql
-- 1) Description: Show the distinct order‑level statuses that exist, so we know which ones represent real purchases.
SELECT DISTINCT "status"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: Count how many orders of each status happened up to the end of 2022.
SELECT "status",
       COUNT(*) AS "orders_through_2022"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
WHERE "created_at" < 1672531200000000
GROUP BY "status"
ORDER BY "orders_through_2022" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: Glimpse at raw timestamps vs. human‑readable form for orders before 2023.
SELECT "order_id",
       "user_id",
       "created_at",
       TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ("created_at"/1e6) AS "order_ts"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
WHERE "created_at" < 1672531200000000
ORDER BY "created_at" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: For each user, pull out the very first purchase (order header view, good‑status only, through 2022).
SELECT "user_id",
       MIN("created_at") AS "first_order_ts_raw",
       TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(MIN("created_at")/1e6) AS "first_order_ts"
FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
WHERE "created_at" < 1672531200000000
  AND "status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
GROUP BY "user_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: See how many NEW customers there were in each first‑purchase month (cohort size).
WITH firsts AS (
  SELECT "user_id",
         DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(MIN("created_at")/1e6)) AS "first_purchase_month"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
  WHERE "created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND "status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
  GROUP BY "user_id"
)
SELECT "first_purchase_month",
       COUNT(DISTINCT "user_id") AS "new_users"
FROM firsts
GROUP BY "first_purchase_month"
ORDER BY "first_purchase_month"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: Generate every user’s purchases (good statuses) plus their *offset* in months since first purchase.
WITH firsts AS (
  SELECT "user_id",
         MIN("created_at") AS "first_created_at"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
  WHERE "created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND "status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
  GROUP BY "user_id"
),
purchases AS (
  SELECT o."user_id",
         o."created_at",
         f."first_created_at",
         DATEDIFF('month',
                  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(f."first_created_at"/1e6)),
                  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(o."created_at"/1e6))) + 1 AS "month_number"  -- 1 = cohort month
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS" o
  JOIN firsts f ON o."user_id" = f."user_id"
  WHERE o."created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND o."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
)
SELECT *
FROM purchases
WHERE "month_number" BETWEEN 1 AND 4
ORDER BY "user_id", "month_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: Count unique users who placed an order in each cohort month (1‑4) *within* the same cohort.
WITH firsts AS (
  SELECT "user_id",
         DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(MIN("created_at")/1e6)) AS "first_purchase_month",
         MIN("created_at") AS "first_created_at"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
  WHERE "created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND "status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
  GROUP BY "user_id"
),
purchases AS (
  SELECT o."user_id",
         f."first_purchase_month",
         DATEDIFF('month',
                  f."first_purchase_month",
                  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(o."created_at"/1e6))) + 1 AS "month_number"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS" o
  JOIN firsts f ON o."user_id" = f."user_id"
  WHERE o."created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND o."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
)
SELECT "first_purchase_month",
       "month_number",
       COUNT(DISTINCT "user_id") AS "active_users"
FROM purchases
WHERE "month_number" BETWEEN 1 AND 4
GROUP BY "first_purchase_month", "month_number"
ORDER BY "first_purchase_month", "month_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: Add cohort size to compute the retention percentage month by month.
WITH firsts AS (
  SELECT "user_id",
         DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(MIN("created_at")/1e6)) AS "first_purchase_month"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
  WHERE "created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND "status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
  GROUP BY "user_id"
),
cohort_size AS (
  SELECT "first_purchase_month",
         COUNT(DISTINCT "user_id") AS "cohort_users"
  FROM firsts
  GROUP BY "first_purchase_month"
),
activity AS (
  SELECT o."user_id",
         f."first_purchase_month",
         DATEDIFF('month',
                  f."first_purchase_month",
                  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(o."created_at"/1e6))) + 1 AS "month_number"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS" o
  JOIN firsts f ON o."user_id" = f."user_id"
  WHERE o."created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND o."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
)
SELECT a."first_purchase_month",
       a."month_number",
       COUNT(DISTINCT a."user_id") AS "active_users",
       c."cohort_users",
       ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT a."user_id")*100.0 / c."cohort_users", 2) AS "pct_of_cohort"
FROM activity a
JOIN cohort_size c
  ON a."first_purchase_month" = c."first_purchase_month"
WHERE a."month_number" BETWEEN 1 AND 4
GROUP BY a."first_purchase_month", a."month_number", c."cohort_users"
ORDER BY a."first_purchase_month", a."month_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: Summarize the retention percentages averaged across all cohorts (overall view for months 1‑4).
WITH firsts AS (
  SELECT "user_id",
         DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(MIN("created_at")/1e6)) AS "first_purchase_month"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
  WHERE "created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND "status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
  GROUP BY "user_id"
),
cohort_size AS (
  SELECT "first_purchase_month",
         COUNT(DISTINCT "user_id") AS "cohort_users"
  FROM firsts
  GROUP BY "first_purchase_month"
),
activity AS (
  SELECT o."user_id",
         f."first_purchase_month",
         DATEDIFF('month',
                  f."first_purchase_month",
                  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(o."created_at"/1e6))) + 1 AS "month_number"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS" o
  JOIN firsts f ON o."user_id" = f."user_id"
  WHERE o."created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND o."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
)
SELECT "month_number",
       ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN a."month_number" BETWEEN 1 AND 4 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)::FLOAT /
             COUNT(*) * 100, 2) AS "overall_pct_users"
FROM activity a
GROUP BY "month_number"
HAVING "month_number" BETWEEN 1 AND 4
ORDER BY "month_number"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: Same as query 8 but expressed as a pivot, giving one row per cohort and separate columns for months 1‑4.
WITH firsts AS (
  SELECT "user_id",
         DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(MIN("created_at")/1e6)) AS "first_purchase_month"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS"
  WHERE "created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND "status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
  GROUP BY "user_id"
),
cohort_size AS (
  SELECT "first_purchase_month",
         COUNT(DISTINCT "user_id") AS "cohort_users"
  FROM firsts
  GROUP BY "first_purchase_month"
),
activity AS (
  SELECT o."user_id",
         f."first_purchase_month",
         DATEDIFF('month',
                  f."first_purchase_month",
                  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(o."created_at"/1e6))) + 1 AS "month_number"
  FROM THELOOK_ECOMMERCE.THELOOK_ECOMMERCE."ORDERS" o
  JOIN firsts f ON o."user_id" = f."user_id"
  WHERE o."created_at" < 1672531200000000
    AND o."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Processing')
    AND DATEDIFF('month',
                  f."first_purchase_month",
                  DATE_TRUNC('month', TO_TIMESTAMP_LTZ(o."created_at"/1e6))) + 1 BETWEEN 1 AND 4
)
SELECT "first_purchase_month",
       ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN "month_number" = 1 THEN "user_id" END) * 100.0 /
             MAX("cohort_users"), 2) AS "m1_pct",
       ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN "month_number" = 2 THEN "user_id" END) * 100.0 /
             MAX("cohort_users"), 2) AS "m2_pct",
       ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN "month_number" = 3 THEN "user_id" END) * 100.0 /
             MAX("cohort_users"), 2) AS "m3_pct",
       ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN "month_number" = 4 THEN "user_id" END) * 100.0 /
             MAX("cohort_users"), 2) AS "m4_pct"
FROM activity
JOIN cohort_size USING("first_purchase_month")
GROUP BY "first_purchase_month"
ORDER BY "first_purchase_month"
LIMIT 20;
```

The ten statements above progress from verifying basic data quality to producing the cohort‑based retention percentages you need, all while staying within the required table set and Snowflake SQL syntax.
